// Deutsch-Jozsa: a provable quantum advantage, in one query.
//
// Concept
//   You are given a black-box function f on 2-bit inputs, promised to be either
//   *constant* (same output for every input) or *balanced* (output 0 for half
//   the inputs and 1 for the other half). Classically, telling which can take
//   3 queries in the worst case. This circuit does it with 1.
//
//   The oracle here implements the balanced function f(x) = x0 XOR x1, built
//   from two CNOTs onto the output qubit.
//
// Expected quantum behaviour
//   The output qubit is prepared in |->, so the oracle writes (-1)^f(x) as a
//   phase onto each input branch instead of changing the output bit. The final
//   Hadamards turn that phase pattern into a definite bit string.
//
// Expected measurement results
//   The input register reads 11 in 100% of shots. A non-zero result proves f is
//   balanced. Compare `deutsch-jozsa-constant.qf`, which reads 00 every time.
//
// Mathematical interpretation
//   After the Hadamards and the oracle the input register holds
//   (1/2) * sum over x of (-1)^f(x) |x>. Applying H to each input qubit gives
//   amplitude (1/4) * sum over x of (-1)^(f(x) + x.z) on |z>.
//
//   For f(x) = x.a with a = 11, the sum is 4 when z = a and 0 otherwise, so the
//   measurement returns the hidden string a with certainty. For constant f the
//   same sum concentrates entirely on z = 00.
//
//   This is the smallest honest example of interference doing computational
//   work: every branch is computed, and the wrong answers cancel.
//
// @expect qubits=3 clbits=2 gates=8
// @expect-outcome 11

qubit[3] q;
bit[2] result;

// q[0], q[1] are the function input; q[2] is the oracle's output qubit.
x q[2];
h q[2];

h q[0];
h q[1];

// Oracle for the balanced function f(x) = x0 XOR x1.
cx q[0], q[2];
cx q[1], q[2];

h q[0];
h q[1];

measure q[0] -> result[0];
measure q[1] -> result[1];
